WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.690
Last year our school used a model to

2
00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:04.310
predict which students would pass.

3
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.720
On last year's students it

4
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.220
was right every time.

5
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.700
This year it was wrong for

6
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.080
one student in three.

7
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.820
Today: why we test a model on

8
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.400
data it has never seen.

9
00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.650
The fix is simple.

10
00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.950
Split your data before you start: about

11
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.570
eighty percent to train the model, and

12
00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.830
twenty percent locked away to test it.

13
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.160
The test set plays the part

14
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.420
of next year's pupils.

15
00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.310
A model can remember its training pupils

16
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.290
without learning anything useful.

17
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.640
The test pupils are new to it, so its

18
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.160
score on them is an honest guess of

19
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.380
how it will do next year.

20
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.960
Here is the test error as we make the

21
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.560
model more and more complex.

22
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.560
It falls at first, then rises again.

23
00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.790
The middle model is the best

24
00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:56.770
one for new pupils.

25
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.490
A model that bends to fit every

26
00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:02.890
training pupil is overfitting.

27
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.390
It learns the noise, like one pupil's

28
00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.570
lucky day, as if it were a rule.

29
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.020
It looks perfect in training

30
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.080
and fails on new data.

31
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.880
One rule matters most.

32
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.630
Never peek at the test set

33
00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.090
while you build the model.

34
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.580
If you tune the model to the test

35
00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.480
pupils, the test stops being fair.

36
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:29.360
Quick check!

37
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.750
A model scores one hundred percent

38
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.830
on its training pupils and sixty

39
00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:37.750
percent on the test pupils.

40
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.450
Which score tells you how

41
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:42.850
it will do next year?

42
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.540
The test score, sixty percent.

43
00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.610
The perfect training score only shows

44
00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.430
the model remembered its pupils.

45
00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:54.730
The big gap is a sign of overfitting.

46
00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.440
In the app, open the overfitting lab.

47
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.400
Raise the complexity slider and

48
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.800
watch the training error fall while

49
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.640
the test error turns back up.

50
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:07.380
Your turn.

51
00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.850
Why do we keep twenty percent of the data

52
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.950
away from the model while we build it?

53
00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:16.590
To check it on pupils it has never seen.

54
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.270
Remember: train on most of the data, test

55
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.250
once on the rest, and a big gap between

56
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.330
the two scores means overfitting.

57
00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.060
Try it yourself: make a rule from ten

58
00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.020
cards, then test it on five hidden cards.

59
00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:34.410
Did your rule still work on the

60
00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:35.690
cards it had never seen?
